.\" Keep in step with print_usage() and the header comment in
.\" src/bin/hkdfguard.rs.
.TH HKDFGUARD 1 2026-10-02 "hkdfguard" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hkdfguard \- provision a service's HKDFGuard KEK and wrap DEKs under it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hkdfguard provision
.BR \-\-service\-name | \-sn
.I name
.PP
.B hkdfguard wrap
.BR \-\-key\-file\-path | \-kf
.I path
.BR \-\-service\-name | \-sn
.I name
.RB ( \-\-dek\-stdin " | " \-\-dek\-file
.IR path )
.RB [ \-\-fingerprint | \-fp
.IR hex ]
.RB [ \-\-force | \-f ]
.PP
.B hkdfguard retire
.BR \-\-service\-name | \-sn
.I name
.BR \-\-fingerprint | \-fp
.I hex
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hkdfguard
sets up and uses the persistent, per-service Key Encryption Key (KEK) of
the HKDFGuard library. The KEK lives on the strongest provider that the
host has and that policy allows: a TPM 2.0, a PKCS#11 token, or an
external secret file.
.TP
.B provision
Ensures a KEK exists for the service, creating one if it does not, and
prints its fingerprint: record it.
Run it once, at deployment time. With
.BR retire ,
it is the only command that makes the library's setup calls, which are
deliberately slow. Under the production security model, creating a KEK
needs root; for an existing one, anyone may run it to verify the KEK.
.TP
.B wrap
Wraps a 32-byte Data Encryption Key (DEK) under the service's existing
KEK and writes the wrapped payload to
.IR \-\-key\-file\-path ,
mode 0600, and prints the fingerprint of the KEK it wrapped under. It
never creates a KEK: if none has been provisioned, it fails and says to run
.BR provision .
Under the production security model it needs root or a deployer, and
refuses anyone else before reading the DEK.
.TP
.B retire
Permanently deletes the service's KEK, only if its fingerprint is the one
given; every DEK wrapped under it can no longer be unwrapped. PKCS#11
only: a TPM KEK is re-derived on demand and not stored, so there is
nothing to delete. Under the production security model it needs root.
.SH SECURITY MODEL
The policy's top-level
.B security_model
setting is
.B production
(the default, also with no policy file) or
.BR developer .
Production splits the duties: root, the security admin, runs
.B provision
and
.BR retire ;
root or a deployer, the deployment admin, runs
.BR wrap ;
the application only unwraps. A deployer is a direct member of the group
.B hkdfguard\-deployers
(the packages create it) or of a group listed in the policy's
.BR deployer_groups .
The library trusts only key files owned by root or a deployer, in folders
owned by root or a deployer and group-writable only through a deployer
group; any other is refused with
.BR HKDFGUARD_ERR_FILE_UNTRUSTED .
Under developer, anyone who can reach the provider may do all three, and
key files owned by the reading account are trusted. Wrapping and
unwrapping themselves are the same under both.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-service\-name ", " \-sn " \fIname\fR"
The service whose KEK is used. At most 128 bytes of ASCII letters,
digits and dots; it must not start with a dot or contain "..".
.TP
.BR \-\-key\-file\-path ", " \-kf " \fIpath\fR"
Where
.B wrap
writes the wrapped payload. Its folder must be a real folder (no
symbolic link anywhere above the file), and neither that folder nor any
folder above it up to
.I /
may be writable by group or others \(em sticky folders such as
.IR /tmp ,
.I /var/tmp
and
.I /dev/shm
included. The library refuses to read a key file under such a folder
.RB ( HKDFGUARD_ERR_FILE_UNTRUSTED ),
so
.B wrap
refuses to write one there, before the DEK is read. Under the developer
security model only the folders' modes are checked, not who owns them;
under production each folder must also be owned by root or a deployer,
and may be group-writable only through a deployer group.
.TP
.B \-\-dek\-stdin
Read the DEK, base64 of exactly 32 bytes, from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-\-dek\-file " path"
Read the DEK, base64 of exactly 32 bytes, from a file. The file must be a
regular file, not a symlink, owned by root or by the invoking user, with
no group or other access (mode 0400 or 0600). Its folder, and every folder
above it up to
.IR / ,
must be a real folder owned by root or the invoking user and not writable
by group or others \(em sticky folders such as
.I /tmp
included, unless on a read-only mount.
.TP
.BR \-\-force ", " \-f
Replace an existing
.IR \-\-key\-file\-path .
The new payload is written to a temporary file in the same folder first,
then renamed over the old file in one step, so a failed wrap or write
never touches it. The replaced file is then overwritten; that cannot
guarantee erasure on copy-on-write filesystems or flash storage.
.TP
.BR \-\-fingerprint ", " \-fp " \fIhex\fR"
The KEK's fingerprint as
.B provision
printed it, 64 hex characters. For
.BR wrap ,
optional: nothing is written unless the DEK was wrapped under that KEK,
and with
.B \-\-force
the existing file is left as it was. For
.BR retire ,
required: nothing is deleted unless it matches.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print usage and exit.
.PP
The DEK is never accepted as a command-line argument or from an
environment variable: other processes of the same user can read both
through
.IR /proc .
A single trailing newline on the DEK input is ignored.
.PP
Each option may be given once, and an option's value may not start with
.BR \- :
.B "wrap \-kf \-\-force"
is an invalid command line, not a key file named
.BR \-\-force .
Write a path that starts with
.B \-
as
.IR ./\-name .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed, or the process could not be hardened (core dumps,
ptrace, memory locking) and refused to handle key material.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hkdfguard/policy.toml
Administrative policy: which providers may be used, and their
configuration. Optional. When present it must be owned by root and not
writable by group or others, and so must every directory above it up to
.IR / ,
sticky ones such as
.I /tmp
included. The same holds for every file it names: nothing the library
reads is trusted anywhere under
.IR /tmp ,
.I /var/tmp
or
.IR /dev/shm ;
only a read-only mount is exempt.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hkdfguard/policy.toml.example
Annotated example policy, every setting commented out at its default.
Copy it to
.I /etc/hkdfguard/policy.toml
to start one.
.TP
.I /etc/hkdfguard/tpm.derivation\-secret
Per-host secret mixed into TPM-derived KEKs.
.TP
.I /etc/hkdfguard/pkcs11.pin
Default PKCS#11 user PIN file.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/sysusers.d/hkdfguard.conf
Creates the groups
.B hkdfguard
and
.BR hkdfguard\-deployers ,
which are never removed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo hkdfguard provision \-sn com.company.orders
printf '%s' "$DEK_B64" | sudo hkdfguard wrap \e
    \-kf /var/lib/app/key.bin \-sn com.company.orders \-\-dek\-stdin \-g app
hkdfguard wrap \-kf /var/lib/app/key.bin \e
    \-sn com.company.orders \-\-dek\-file "$CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY/dek"
hkdfguard retire \-sn com.company.orders \-fp <fingerprint from provision>
.fi
.PP
.B printf
is a shell builtin, so the DEK never appears in a process's argument
list; an external
.B echo
would put it there.
.SH SEE ALSO
The HKDFGuard README, installed under
.IR /usr/share/doc/ ,
documents providers, the policy file and deployment.
